WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.886
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.186 --> 00:00:08.839
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.139 --> 00:00:16.784
Unit sixteen: Environmental Policy. Today you will evaluate instruments using effective for / effective at + gerund.

4
00:00:17.084 --> 00:00:19.771
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.733 --> 00:00:24.129
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.983 --> 00:00:26.902
Environmental policy.

7
00:00:27.917 --> 00:00:32.064
Environmental policy means government action to protect the environment.

8
00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:36.972
Environmental policy must balance cost and benefit.

9
00:00:37.583 --> 00:00:40.909
Your turn. Say: Environmental policy.

10
00:00:43.767 --> 00:00:45.987
Environmental policy.

11
00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:47.937
Carbon tax.

12
00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:52.807
Carbon tax means a charge on each unit of greenhouse gas emitted.

13
00:00:53.417 --> 00:00:56.182
A carbon tax raises the price of pollution.

14
00:00:56.783 --> 00:00:59.958
Your turn. Say: Carbon tax.

15
00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:04.219
Carbon tax.

16
00:01:04.983 --> 00:01:06.135
Regulation.

17
00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:11.181
Regulation means an official rule that governs how something may be done.

18
00:01:11.783 --> 00:01:14.596
Regulation limited emissions from factories.

19
00:01:15.183 --> 00:01:18.010
Your turn. Say: Regulation.

20
00:01:20.867 --> 00:01:22.077
Regulation.

21
00:01:22.817 --> 00:01:24.085
Externality.

22
00:01:25.083 --> 00:01:29.672
Externality means a cost or benefit that falls on people who did not choose it.

23
00:01:30.283 --> 00:01:32.469
Pollution is a negative externality.

24
00:01:33.083 --> 00:01:35.759
Your turn. Say: Externality.

25
00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:39.891
Externality.

26
00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:41.779
Conservation.

27
00:01:42.783 --> 00:01:47.058
Conservation means the protection of natural resources and habitats.

28
00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:49.661
Conservation preserved the wetland.

29
00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:52.972
Your turn. Say: Conservation.

30
00:01:55.833 --> 00:01:56.869
Conservation.

31
00:01:57.617 --> 00:01:58.908
Sustainability.

32
00:01:59.917 --> 00:02:04.296
Sustainability means meeting present needs without harming future generations.

33
00:02:04.883 --> 00:02:07.196
Sustainability guided the city's plan.

34
00:02:07.783 --> 00:02:10.482
Your turn. Say: Sustainability.

35
00:02:13.333 --> 00:02:14.648
Sustainability.

36
00:02:15.383 --> 00:02:16.849
Policy instrument.

37
00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:21.893
Policy instrument means a tool such as a tax, rule or subsidy.

38
00:02:22.483 --> 00:02:25.284
A policy instrument should match the problem.

39
00:02:25.883 --> 00:02:29.255
Your turn. Say: Policy instrument.

40
00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:33.554
Policy instrument.

41
00:02:34.317 --> 00:02:36.154
Cost-benefit analysis.

42
00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:41.866
Cost-benefit analysis means comparing the total costs and benefits of a policy.

43
00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:45.204
Cost-benefit analysis supported the new rule.

44
00:02:45.817 --> 00:02:49.212
Your turn. Say: Cost-benefit analysis.

45
00:02:52.067 --> 00:02:54.554
Cost-benefit analysis.

46
00:02:55.367 --> 00:02:59.087
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:03.912
Visal, should the government ban polluting factories or simply tax them?

48
00:03:05.017 --> 00:03:14.623
Each instrument has strengths. A carbon tax can be effective at reducing emissions because it gives firms a financial reason to cut.

49
00:03:15.717 --> 00:03:16.996
And regulation?

50
00:03:18.083 --> 00:03:27.783
Regulation is effective for setting firm limits, particularly where harm is severe. A tax is effective at encouraging flexibility.

51
00:03:28.883 --> 00:03:30.024
Which is better?

52
00:03:31.117 --> 00:03:40.468
Cost-benefit analysis helps decide. Often a mix is effective at achieving targets while keeping costs manageable.

53
00:03:41.633 --> 00:03:43.238
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:44.083 --> 00:03:48.115
Grammar focus: Effective for and effective at plus gerund.

55
00:03:48.415 --> 00:03:49.613
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:50.317 --> 00:03:53.744
A carbon tax can be effective at reducing emissions.

57
00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:57.404
Regulation is effective for setting firm limits.

58
00:03:58.317 --> 00:04:01.872
A policy mix is effective at achieving targets cheaply.

59
00:04:02.833 --> 00:04:12.266
effective at / effective in / effective for + gerund or noun: effective at reducing, effective for emissions control. Never use an infinitive after the preposition.

60
00:04:13.017 --> 00:04:17.422
Be careful! Don't say: A tax is effective at reduce emissions.

61
00:04:19.367 --> 00:04:22.249
Say: A tax is effective at reducing emissions.

62
00:04:22.549 --> 00:04:23.968
at + gerund.

63
00:04:24.767 --> 00:04:27.349
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:28.133 --> 00:04:29.518
Choosing the Right Tool.

65
00:04:29.818 --> 00:04:34.963
Environmental policy offers governments several instruments for cutting pollution.

66
00:04:35.967 --> 00:04:43.086
A carbon tax can be effective at reducing emissions because it makes polluters pay for the damage, an externality that markets ignore.

67
00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:50.244
Regulation is effective for setting firm limits, though it may be less flexible and more costly to administer.

68
00:04:51.233 --> 00:04:57.726
Conservation and subsidies for clean technology are effective at protecting ecosystems and speeding innovation.

69
00:04:58.733 --> 00:05:07.339
Cost-benefit analysis helps policymakers compare instruments, although some values, such as biodiversity, are hard to price.

70
00:05:08.433 --> 00:05:12.249
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:13.083 --> 00:05:16.221
A carbon tax can be effective at blank emissions.

72
00:05:16.521 --> 00:05:20.906
A, reducing. B, reduce. C, to reduce.

73
00:05:24.467 --> 00:05:26.547
The answer is A: reducing.

74
00:05:27.683 --> 00:05:31.053
A cost that falls on people who did not choose it is an blank.

75
00:05:31.353 --> 00:05:35.378
A, externality. B, instrument. C, analysis.

76
00:05:38.933 --> 00:05:40.956
The answer is A: externality.

77
00:05:42.117 --> 00:05:44.824
Comparing total costs and benefits is blank.

78
00:05:45.124 --> 00:05:50.914
A, conservation. B, cost-benefit analysis. C, regulation.

79
00:05:54.467 --> 00:05:57.534
The answer is B: cost-benefit analysis.

80
00:05:58.683 --> 00:06:00.416
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:06:00.716 --> 00:06:08.828
A, Rules are effective for limiting waste.. B, Rules are effective for limit waste.. C, Rules are effective to limiting waste..

82
00:06:12.367 --> 00:06:15.678
The answer is A: Rules are effective for limiting waste..

83
00:06:16.817 --> 00:06:19.513
According to the reading, some values are hard to…

84
00:06:19.813 --> 00:06:23.513
A, tax. B, price. C, protect.

85
00:06:27.067 --> 00:06:29.078
The answer is B: price.

86
00:06:30.267 --> 00:06:33.465
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:34.317 --> 00:06:38.499
What policy instrument do you think is most effective for protecting the environment?

88
00:06:55.283 --> 00:06:57.445
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:57.745 --> 00:07:19.487
A carbon tax seems to me the most effective instrument for reducing emissions, because it uses prices to change behaviour across the whole economy. It works best with support for low-income households and with regulation where harm is severe. No single tool is enough, but pricing pollution corrects the externality directly.

90
00:07:19.787 --> 00:07:21.601
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:22.517 --> 00:07:27.619
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and effective for and effective at plus gerund.

92
00:07:27.919 --> 00:07:34.275
Today's value is environmental responsibility. Small green actions at home help the whole planet.

93
00:07:34.575 --> 00:07:37.564
See you in Unit seventeen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:37.864 --> 00:07:40.690
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

